**Idea.** An LRU cache evicts the least recently used entry when full. Use a `HashMap<K, Node>` for O(1) lookup and a doubly linked list ordered by recency (head = most recent, tail = least recent) for O(1) move and remove. Dummy head and tail nodes remove null checks.

**Generic implementation.**

    import java.util.*;

    class LRUCache<K, V> {
        private class Node {
            K key; V val; Node prev, next;
            Node(K k, V v) { key = k; val = v; }
        }
        private final int capacity;
        private final Map<K, Node> map = new HashMap<>();
        private final Node head = new Node(null, null), tail = new Node(null, null);

        LRUCache(int capacity) {
            this.capacity = capacity;
            head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;
        }
        V get(K key) {
            Node n = map.get(key);
            if (n == null) return null;
            remove(n); addFront(n);
            return n.val;
        }
        void put(K key, V val) {
            Node n = map.get(key);
            if (n != null) { n.val = val; remove(n); addFront(n); return; }
            if (map.size() == capacity) {
                Node lru = tail.prev;
                remove(lru); map.remove(lru.key);
            }
            n = new Node(key, val);
            map.put(key, n); addFront(n);
        }
        private void remove(Node n) { n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev; }
        private void addFront(Node n) {
            n.next = head.next; n.prev = head;
            head.next.prev = n; head.next = n;
        }
    }

**Why it works.** `get` and `put` do a constant number of hash lookups and pointer updates, so each is O(1). The node stores its key so the map entry can be removed on eviction. Hit: move to front. Insert when full: evict `tail.prev`.

**Edge cases.** Capacity of 1, updating an existing key (must not evict), `get` on a missing key, and capacity 0 or negative (reject in the constructor).

**Shortcut to mention:** in Java, `LinkedHashMap` with `accessOrder=true` and an overridden `removeEldestEntry` gives LRU in a few lines, but interviewers usually want the manual version.

**Thread safety:** simplest is `synchronized` methods, since even `get` mutates the list; for higher throughput use sharding or a library such as Caffeine.
